  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Interface Density**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user\'s issue, which clearly revolves around interface density. They\'re struggling with the toolbar and sidebar consuming too much screen real estate, overshadowing the actual content. I\'m exploring the problem\'s roots, with \"Google\" being a good starting point.\n\n\n**Analyzing Screen Real Estate**\n\nI\'ve quantified the space the toolbars and sidebar occupy. The menu bar, toolbar, and padding consume over 160px of vertical space. The sidebar is a whopping 288px wide. The document preview area is using too much padding as well. I\'m aiming for a solution: compact mode. This involves a toggle for a slim header bar combining the menu and toolbar, perhaps with a \"hamburger\" menu.\n\n\n**Designing Compact Interfaces**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a clearer picture for interface compactness. Slim buttons, 28px high with icons and tooltips, are the goal for the toolbar, which recovers over 100 vertical pixels. Standard mode uses 32px buttons with labels toggleable. Finally, I\'m envisioning a Zen/Focus fullscreen mode with a minimalist floating top pill or auto-hiding bar for maximum document space.\n\n**Refining Sidebar Options**\n\nNow I\'m designing the sidebar\'s compact modes. Instead of just open or closed, I\'m thinking of a Mini Icon Rail mode, around 48px wide, displaying document icons with hover tooltips. This is the first of several more compact modes.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 557673624
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
