  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering User Experience**\n\nI\'m now delving into the user experience, specifically focusing on optimizing the interface\'s compactness. The goal is to address the feedback regarding the toolbar and sidebar\'s dominance over the main content area, and how they should be made less imposing. Providing an option for control seems essential.\n\n\n**Analyzing UI Space Usage**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the core UI issue: excessive use of vertical and horizontal screen space. The top menu and toolbar, especially, consume nearly half the height, and the sidebar claims significant horizontal width. I\'m now developing a solution centered on a \"Compact UI Mode\" toggle with three states: \'compact\', \'standard\', and \'zen\', and fast toggle buttons for quick access. This will allow the user to easily control their visible work space.\n\n\n**Refining Interface States**\n\nI\'m now refining the Compact UI Mode. I\'m imagining a streamlined approach with quick toggle buttons for rapid access to the three states. The \"Compact Mode\" would feature a consolidated single-row header, slimming down menus and using sleek icons. \"Zen Mode\" offers a distraction-free experience with a minimalist floating control, dedicating almost all the screen to the document. \"Standard Mode\" keeps a modern look.\n\n\n**Developing Sidebar Modes**\n\nI\'m now exploring the sidebar states. I\'ve designed three modes: compact (mini-rail), expanded (full drawer), and hidden (collapsed). The compact mode is a mini-rail with document icons and a quick \"new page\" button, while the expanded mode provides the full tree view. The hidden state offers a toggle for complete visibility. I am working on the quick toggles for rapid state switching. I\'m also reducing redundant padding on the document canvas to maximize editing space.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 629363799
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
